Each producer (Append, Overwrite, Delete, RowDelta, RewriteDataFiles) runs a set of validators before the commit lands. They check that the files the operation assumed (added, deleted, or filtered) still match the current table state. A failed validation surfaces as one of: table.ErrCommitFailed — the base snapshot moved on; the commit can be retried after refreshing (see below). table.ErrCommitDiverged — terminal. The base snapshot is no longer on the branch at all, so a retry cannot reconcile the change. table.ErrDataFilesMissing — terminal. Data files that the operation references, such as the target of a position delete or deletion vector, are not live, so retrying the same delete cannot succeed. Rebuild the delete against the current table. When Transaction.Commit returns it, the error also matches ErrTransactionUnusable. table.ErrDuplicateDataFiles — terminal for the requested paths. A RowDelta rejects repeated paths during staging and checks whether a concurrent commit added any of its paths that are still live on retry. AddDataFiles and AddDataFilesByPath make the same retry check unless the duplicate check is disabled (WithoutDuplicateCheck or ignoreDuplicates). A retry collision also matches ErrTransactionUnusable. Use distinct paths in a new operation. table.ErrTransactionUnusable — this transaction can't be committed again. If the error also matches ErrCommitFailed, retry in a new transaction from a reloaded table. import ( "context" "errors" "github.com/apache/iceberg-go/table"
) _, err := txn.Commit(context.Background())
switch {
case errors.Is(err, table.ErrDuplicateDataFiles): // choose different data file paths before building a new RowDelta
case errors.Is(err, table.ErrTransactionUnusable) && errors.Is(err, table.ErrCommitFailed): // a concurrent change: retry in a new transaction from a reloaded table
case errors.Is(err, table.ErrCommitDiverged), errors.Is(err, table.ErrTransactionUnusable): // unrecoverable: rebuild the operation from the latest table
case errors.Is(err, table.ErrCommitFailed): // retriable: refresh and try again (the retry loop does this for you)
case errors.Is(err, table.ErrCommitRejected): // the catalog wrote nothing: fix the cause, then call Commit again
} A commit can also fail with table.ErrCommitRejected, which means the catalog rejected it before writing anything, for example an update the catalog can't apply or a REST 400, 401, 403 or 404. The commit is not retried, and the transaction can still be committed.

