On each retry, iceberg-go reloads the table metadata, applies staged changes in order and runs the producer's validators against the fresh snapshot. It submits the commit only if it is still valid. An append staged after a rollback is rebuilt on the rollback target. RollbackToSnapshot, AssertRefSnapshotID, and CherryPickSnapshot pin the refs they guard. If a pinned ref moves, the commit fails instead of rewriting that assertion to the new head. Cherry-picks can retry table-level conflicts when their pinned refs stay unchanged. A divergent replay onto a new branch also pins main; a fast-forward onto a missing branch points directly to the source snapshot and does not depend on main. Each replayed snapshot from a staged write gets the current timestamp, or one millisecond after its parent if that is later. Its timestamp reflects the attempt that commits it, not staging time. RollbackToSnapshot, AssertRefSnapshotID, FastForwardBranch, ReplaceBranchFromRef, and ReplaceBranch pin the refs they guard. Promoting from a ref pins both the source and target. If the source points to a snapshot staged in the same transaction, promotion also pins the commit branch, preventing retry from rebuilding that source on a different history. If a pinned ref moves, the commit fails instead of rebasing a co-staged append. ExpireSnapshots does not pin refs. Retries replan it as described in Snapshot expiration . Commit retries a conflict up to 4 times by default (commit.retry.num-retries is 4, as in Java and PyIceberg). Set it to 0 to fail on the first conflict; see Manifest and commit in the configuration reference for the other retry properties: // Fail on the first conflict instead of retrying.
_, err := cat.CreateTable(ctx, ident, schema, catalog.WithProperties(iceberg.Properties{ "commit.retry.num-retries": "0", }),
) Catalog support: the REST, SQL, Glue, Hive, and Hadoop catalogs report commit conflicts, including requirements that fail because of a concurrent change, as ErrCommitFailed, so the retry loop engages on all of them. A commit on another branch that takes the staged snapshot's sequence number or first-row-id is a conflict too, and the retry renumbers the snapshot. Snapshot-ID assertions cannot detect concurrent retention-only edits. REST catalogs also cannot detect a ref type change that leaves its snapshot ID unchanged. Avoid editing ref retention concurrently with branch promotion. Only the implicit assertion on the commit branch's head is rebased after a refresh. Other requirements, including the schema, spec, sort order, and pinned refs, must still hold. Otherwise, the commit fails without another attempt and the transaction is unusable. After a conflict, calling Commit again refreshes the table and replays staged changes even with retries disabled. Each call gets the configured retry budget. A commit that removes delete files and cannot be replayed resubmits only if its branch has not moved. A staged change that cannot apply to the refreshed table makes the transaction unusable. For example, a staged format upgrade cannot apply if a peer has already committed a higher format version. The error keeps its cause, so it also matches ErrCommitFailed when the cause is a conflict. See ErrTransactionUnusable for all conditions that end the transaction after refresh. A failed commit keeps the files it staged until a later commit lands. A transaction you abandon leaves them for orphan file removal, as one with retries disabled always has. An abandoned manifest rewrite also leaves the manifests that later attempts merged. TableCommit refuses a transaction whose commit failed, since the multi-table path does not refresh. Call Commit to resume it, or build a new transaction.

